15:07:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The difference isn't really that GPUs find one task "easy" and the other "hard" — it's that games and pre-rendered animation operate under completely different constraints. **Time budget is the core issue** - **Games** need to render a new frame roughly every 16 milliseconds (for 60 fps) or less. Everything—lighting, shadows, physics, AI—has to happen in that tiny window, in real time, in response to whatever the player does. - **Movies/shows** aren't rendered live. A single frame of a Pixar movie might take **hours** to render on a render farm with thousands of computers working in parallel. There's no player interaction to account for, so the same frame can be computed well ahead of time. **Why pre-rendered animation can afford more detail** - Studios use techniques like **path tracing** and **ray tracing** with huge numbers of light bounces, complex shaders, and high-resolution textures/geometry, because they can throw as much time and hardware at each frame as needed. - If a frame looks wrong, artists can go back and re-render it. There's no requirement to compute it "just in time." **Why games have to cut corners** - Game engines rely on tricks and approximations to *fake* realistic lighting and detail fast enough: baked lighting, simplified shadow maps, level-of-detail scaling, rasterization instead of full ray tracing, upscaling (like DLSS), etc. - Real-time ray tracing exists now, but it's often limited to specific effects (reflections, some shadows) because full path tracing at 60+ fps is still extremely demanding. - Games also have to render an unpredictable range of scenarios (any camera angle, any player action), whereas a movie only needs to render the exact shots the director chose. **In short** It's not that GPUs are bad at real-time rendering — it's that real-time rendering has a much stricter deadline and less room for heavy computation, while pre-rendered animation can spend virtually unlimited time and computing power per frame since it's all computed in advance, frame by frame, off-line.
