ling out with more uniform CPU nodes. 4. **Type of scenes**: An individual Blender artist's personal projects, however impressive, are usually far less complex than blockbuster movie shots involving simulations of oceans, explosions, crowds, hair/fur systems, etc. — these can demand memory and processing that push against GPU limitations. **Bottom line** Your buddy isn't wrong at all — GPU rendering really is faster for a huge amount of work, including plenty of professional and semi-professional 3D projects. What's changed is that **GPUs have become increasingly viable and often preferred for many rendering tasks**, including in some big productions now. But major studios doing the biggest, most complex VFX work still often rely on hybrid CPU/GPU setups, partly due to memory constraints and partly due to established pipelines. Both trends — "GPU rendering is often dramatically faster" and "large-scale studio rendering is still frequently hybrid CPU/GPU" — are true simultaneously.
